// Pixel / sample OUTPUT — the inverse of `media`, and the sibling of `speech`.
// See design.md D11.
//
// Part of the `pie:inferlet` package. The vendored copies live under the
// builds' `deps/` trees (kept in sync via `scripts/sync-wit.sh` / `wit-deps`);
// this file is the source of truth.
//
// THE POINT OF THIS INTERFACE IS THAT PIXELS DO NOT CROSS INTO WASM. A decode
// stage hands the guest a `frames` handle the way `media.image` hands it a
// preprocessed span: the payload stays host-side (host memory today, a device
// pointer once the VAE writes one), and the guest holds a handle plus the
// facts it needs to size a job — width, height, count, fps.
//
// Two doors lead out of a handle, and they are not the same door:
//
//   * `encode(format)` returns the encoded bytes AS A GUEST VALUE. That is a
//     copy into linear memory, taken deliberately, for a guest that wants to
//     hash the file, splice it into a multipart body, or hand it to
//     `wasi:filesystem`. A 4K clip through this door is a 4K clip in the
//     guest's heap.
//   * `session.send-frames` encodes and streams to the client WITHOUT the
//     bytes ever being materialized in linear memory. This is the door the
//     happy path uses, and the reason the resource exists rather than a
//     `list<u8>`-returning function.
//
// `from-rgb8` is the host-side constructor: a guest (or a test) that has raw
// interleaved RGB8 can build a handle before any VAE exists. It is the only
// way to MAKE one today; every other producer is a host reading.
interface frames {

    use types.{error, blob};

    // How a `frames` handle is serialized.
    //
    // `png`, `jpeg` and `webp` are STILLS: they are valid only when
    // `count() == 1`, and a multi-frame handle refuses them by name rather
    // than silently encoding frame 0. `webp` is the lossless VP8L encoder
    // (pure Rust); it is not the animated form.
    //
    // `raw-rgb8` is the parity format — exactly the bytes the handle holds,
    // `count * height * width * 3`, no header — so a test can compare against
    // what it put in. `y4m` is the parity format for a clip: an uncompressed
    // sequence any decoder reads, which is what makes an mp4 regression
    // provable rather than merely visible.
    //
    // `mp4-h264` is the real video format: H.264 High profile from NVENC,
    // muxed into ISO base media (avc1 + avcC). It needs an NVIDIA encoder on
    // the machine and a runtime built with the CUDA shell; without either it
    // refuses by name, saying which half is missing.
    enum image-format {
        png,
        jpeg,
        webp,
        raw-rgb8,
        y4m,
        mp4-h264,
    }

    // How a `pcm` handle is serialized. `wav` is 16-bit PCM RIFF (the form
    // every player takes); `raw-f32` is the parity format — the samples as
    // little-endian f32, interleaved, no header.
    enum audio-format {
        wav,
        raw-f32,
    }

    // A host-side handle to decoded pixels: `count` frames of `width` x
    // `height` interleaved RGB8, in presentation order.
    //
    // Self-describing, like `speech`: the guest never hardcodes a model's
    // latent geometry or frame rate, it asks. A still image is a handle with
    // `count() == 1`; `fps()` is then meaningless and reads 0.
    resource frames {

        // Build a handle from raw interleaved RGB8. `bytes` must be exactly
        // `count * height * width * 3` long; every other length is refused by
        // name with both figures.
        //
        // This is the constructor that lets a test, or a guest holding pixels
        // it made itself, reach the encoders before a VAE decode reading
        // exists to produce a handle the other way.
        from-rgb8: static func(bytes: blob,
                               width: u32,
                               height: u32,
                               count: u32,
                               fps: f32) -> result<frames, error>;

        // Frame width in pixels.
        width: func() -> u32;

        // Frame height in pixels.
        height: func() -> u32;

        // Number of frames. 1 for a still.
        count: func() -> u32;

        // Frames per second. 0 for a still, and for a clip whose producer
        // stated no rate.
        fps: func() -> f32;

        // Encode into `format` and return the bytes AS A GUEST VALUE — a copy
        // into linear memory. Prefer `session.send-frames` when the bytes are
        // headed for the client and nothing in the guest reads them.
        //
        // Errors: a still format against a multi-frame handle, `mp4-h264`
        // without an NVIDIA encoder, or an encoder failure — each by name.
        encode: func(format: image-format) -> result<blob, error>;
    }

    // A host-side handle to decoded audio: interleaved f32 samples in
    // [-1, 1] at `rate` Hz. The audio half of the same statute — the samples
    // stay host-side and `session.send-pcm` streams the encoded file out
    // without them entering linear memory.
    resource pcm {

        // Build a handle from interleaved f32 samples. `channels` must divide
        // the sample count.
        from-f32: static func(samples: list<f32>,
                              rate: u32,
                              channels: u32) -> result<pcm, error>;

        // Sample rate in Hz.
        rate: func() -> u32;

        // Channel count (interleaved).
        channels: func() -> u32;

        // Encode into `format` and return the bytes as a guest value. Total,
        // because both formats are a header plus a transcode of samples the
        // handle already holds: there is nothing here that can fail.
        encode: func(format: audio-format) -> blob;
    }
}
